Translation patch: <EdwardsPoint as Sum<T>>::sum (src/edwards.rs)

Change: `iter.fold(EdwardsPoint::identity(), |acc, item| acc + item.borrow())` becomes
`let mut acc = EdwardsPoint::identity(); while let Some(item) = iter.next() { acc = acc + item.borrow(); } acc`.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided methods
(`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`, `skip`, `size_hint`, `by_ref`, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.

Behaviour: identical for every iterator whose `fold` is consistent with its `next`, i.e. every std
iterator and every lawful iterator. The default `Iterator::fold` is exactly this loop
(`let mut acc = init; while let Some(x) = self.next() { acc = f(acc, x) } acc`): same
`next()` calls, one `borrow()` per item in the same order, each item dropped at the same
point, same panics and termination. `I` may override `fold` (std adapters such as `Map`,
`Chain`, `Rev`, `vec::IntoIter` do; their overrides visit the same items in the same order);
the patched code calls only `next()`, so an iterator whose overridden `fold` disagrees
with its own `next` would give a different result (a logic error in that iterator).
Note: clippy reports `while_let_on_iterator` and `assign_op_pattern` on the new loop.

Facts relied on (not visible in the diff): none.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

diff --git a/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs b/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs
index 1120e8f..376adf0 100644
--- a/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs
+++ b/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs
@@ -838,14 +838,18 @@ impl<T> Sum<T> for EdwardsPoint
 where
     T: Borrow<EdwardsPoint>,
 {
-    fn sum<I>(iter: I) -> Self
+    fn sum<I>(mut iter: I) -> Self
     where
         I: Iterator<Item = T>,
     {
-        iter.fold(EdwardsPoint::identity(), |acc, item| acc + item.borrow())
+        let mut acc = EdwardsPoint::identity();
+        while let Some(item) = iter.next() {
+            acc = acc + item.borrow();
+        }
+        acc
     }
 }
 
